      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) therapy has been reported to improve relapses of asthma following emergency room (ER) discharge, but the benefits of ICS prescription at discharge from the ER are unclear."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess background of the patients who visit ER, and determine whether the prescription of ICS at ER reduces relapses in the patients with asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A prospective multi-center cohort study was conducted at 32 Japanese ERs. Patients aged two to 82 years who visited ER with moderate to severe exacerbation of asthma underwent questionnaire and follow-up three and six months later. The primary observations were of the asthma-related hospitalizations, ER visits, and unscheduled visits."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Among 343 asthma patients (Children: 120, Adults: 223), 33% of children and 12% of adult patients were classified as intermittent asthma, retrospectively. The patients with intermittent had the same rates of hospitalization (29.6%) and ER visits (46.2%) as those with persistent (hospitalization rate: 24.7%, ER visits: 53.1%, ns) before enrolled. The odds ratio for hospitalization in the patients received ICS at the discharge from the ER, compared with the patients who did not received ICS, yielded 0.256 (95% confidence interval, 0.069 to 0.942; p=0.040) by multinomial logistic regression model."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Prescribing ICS at discharge from the ER to asthma patients is associated with the reduction of risk for asthma-related hospitalization. For patients discharged from the ER, including mild intermittent asthma, ICS might be prescribed."
        }
      ]},
